  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: High latitude Rex block across much of \nCanada impacting the pattern across the northern tier of states: low \npressure portion of the block centered over Hudson Bay extends south \ninto the northern Plains/upper Midwest/Great Lakes.  Upper level \nhighs centered over the southwest U.S. and off the mid Atlantic \ncoast...with the remnants of an old low still meandering near the \nFlorida panhandle.  Several short wave troughs rotating through the \nnorther tier cyclonic flow; one crossing the upper Lakes this \nafternoon...a second digging southeast across the Dakotas...and a \ncouple of embedded circulations over northern Saskatchewan and \nnorthwest Ontario.  Northwest-southeast oriented moisture gradient \nacross the upper Lakes: precipitable water values at or below 0.75 \ninch across Upper Michigan...and near 1.50 inches across southeast \nLower.  At the surface...lingering boundary is draped across \nsouthern Lower Michigan...extending southwest across Illinois/ \nnorthern Missouri to a 1014mb frontal wave over eastern Kansas. \nBroad subtropical high over the western Atlantic keeping the \nhumidity up into southern Lower Michigan but as mentioned earlier \nlow levels dry out a bit to the west and north.   \n\nGreat Lakes will remain on the southern periphery of the upper low \nin the vicinity of Hudson Bay through the weekend...with height \nfalls/short wave trough and cold frontal passage expected Friday \nnight/Saturday. A little more influence from zonal Pacific-origin \nflow Sunday...though there will still be short waves zipping by \nalong the jet axis.  Given this zonal flow set up there should be a \npersistent east-west frontal boundary that sets up likely south of \nthe Great Lakes for most of the extended forecast period.  Forecast \ntrends suggest the upper low may make a more concerted push \nsouthward around this time next week. \n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns: \n\nWeakly forced convection overnight/Friday?: Beyond anything that \npops along the Lake Huron breeze this afternoon...some subtle things \nto watch tonight across the southeast third of the forecast area or \nso.  Low level theta-e advection/convergence and elevated CAPE \n(>500J/kg MUCAPE) may be just enough for some scattered convection \nto pop.  Probabilities may be boosted by weak mid level short wave \ntrough/PV anomaly overnight into Friday morning.  Low probability \nevent but probably not low enough to leave out of the forecast. \nDiurnal heating during the day Friday and increasing low level \nmoisture will result in MLCAPE values at or above 1500J/kg \ndeveloping.  Forcing is pretty minimal outside of localized \nconvergence and Lake Huron lake breeze.  Any storms that do develop \nwill have a pulsey-downburst wind threat with them. \n\nPrecipitation chances along passing cold front Friday night/ \nSaturday morning...and more showers Sunday: A better organized/more \nstrongly forced band of showers/thunderstorms expected to cross \nnorthern Michigan later Friday night/Saturday morning before the \nforecast trends drier for Saturday afternoon.  Respite from \nprecipitation chances look to be short...combination of an \napproaching jet streak and increasing low level theta-e advection \nwill bring a renewed threat for showers/thunderstorms late Saturday \nnight through Sunday.\n\nRain continues Monday (heavy rain possibility Lower Michigan): \nFrontal boundary to the south of the Great Lakes with a low\nlevel deformation axis across the upper Lakes and a few ripples\nin the upper level flow will increase the chances for more\nwidespread rain Monday. Lots of mesoscale details to work\nout...but QPF probabilities across northern Lower showing some\npotential (20-40%) of exceeding 1 inch with some low\nprobabilities exceeding 2 inches Monday through Tuesday morning.",
